      "side": 1,
      "message": "Full transparency, I took this from the old code:\nhttps://source.corp.google.com/h/android/platform/superproject/main/+/main:packages/modules/Bluetooth/android/app/src/com/android/bluetooth/pbapclient/obex/PhonebookPullRequest.java;l\u003d58-61\n\nThe pattern in the old code was, more or less, to interrupt the download thread when it was time to disconnect, via abort():\nhttps://source.corp.google.com/h/android/platform/superproject/main/+/main:packages/modules/Bluetooth/android/app/src/com/android/bluetooth/pbapclient/obex/PbapClientConnectionHandler.java;l\u003d323-328\n\nThis can happen after a graceful disconnect timeout:\nhttps://source.corp.google.com/h/android/platform/superproject/main/+/main:packages/modules/Bluetooth/android/app/src/com/android/bluetooth/pbapclient/PbapClientStateMachine.java;l\u003d262\n\nOr, when the state machine is forcibly quit:\nhttps://source.corp.google.com/h/android/platform/superproject/main/+/main:packages/modules/Bluetooth/android/app/src/com/android/bluetooth/pbapclient/PbapClientStateMachine.java;l\u003d373\n\nWhen the thread is interrupted, IO operations are interrupted and fail to work anymore. If we\u0027re mid-IO operation, we get an exception. If we\u0027re not, then we used the isInterrupted() check to see if we were about to fail. In reality, this isn\u0027t a fool proof check, and if timing isn\u0027t _just_ right, we\u0027ll likely to get an exception to handle anyways.\n\nAs for _why_ we do this, I think its because the only other tool we have as a client is to use an OBEX abort command, which the server can reject or not support. If we can\u0027t cancel a potentially long running operation, then we might be slow to disconnect. Interrupting and closing the transport is far more predictable.",
